  "skills": [
    {
      "category": "characterization",
      "trigger": "When evaluating a pulmonary infiltrate or opacity on chest imaging to determine its etiology and clinical relevance.",
      "steps": [
        "Inspect the margins of the pulmonary opacity: ill-defined, blurred, or hazy borders typically suggest an active infectious or inflammatory process (e.g., pneumonia), whereas well-circumscribed, smooth, or calcified margins suggest benign, chronic, or vascular etiologies.",
        "Verify the anatomical location (e.g., right middle lobe vs. left lower lobe) against standard landmarks to ensure the finding matches the described pathology.",
        "Correlate the characterized lesion with the patient's specific symptoms; if the infiltrate is peripheral and infectious in appearance, it is likely unrelated to central airway symptoms like dysphonia, which would require laryngeal or mediastinal involvement."
      ]
    },
    {
      "category": "localization",
      "trigger": "When distinguishing between lung lobes or segments on axial chest CT to validate a reported finding.",
      "steps": [
        "Identify the slice level relative to key structures (e.g., trachea, carina, heart); upper slices showing the trachea and great vessels correspond to upper/middle lobe anatomy, while lower slices showing the ventricles correspond to lower lobes.",
        "Check for the presence of fissures or specific bronchovascular patterns that define lobar boundaries (e.g., the horizontal fissure for the right middle lobe).",
        "Reject options describing findings in lobes not visible at the current slice level or inconsistent with the visible anatomy (e.g., ruling out left lower lobe pathology when viewing the upper thorax)."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
```

